# EdgeForge technical report

> Interim until the final audit. Rendered from `artifacts/metrics/` by `uv run edgeforge docs render`; every number below comes from a metrics file listed in the provenance table at the end. Each result carries one of three labels: **Measured** (realised outcomes of a held-out window), **Simulated** (a model-generated quantity, or a simulation of a bettor), **Assumed** (an assumption stated in a decision record).

## 0. Scope, data and discipline

- **Football only.** Two data sources: football-data.co.uk (results and bookmaker odds, five leagues; the pre-match market comparison uses the 2024/25 season, replication 2025/26) and StatsBomb Open Data for one season, 2015/16 (lineups, minutes, shots, goals, events; players, props, simulator, lineup shock, in-play).
- **Temporal integrity.** Every feature row carries an as-of time; a prior match may feed a feature only if it was complete (latest kick-off plus three hours) by then (D-035). Validation is walk-forward by date. The 2015/16 player block is split by matchweek: burn-in 1 to 9, tuning 10 to 19, test 20 to 38 (D-033). From Phase 4 on, every tuning grid must end interior or be widened before any test metric is computed (D-040) and calibrator adoption is decided inside the tuning window (D-043). **Known violations before that:** the promoted-team prior grid and the player rolling-baseline grid were widened after their test numbers were seen, and the Pillar A calibrators were first adopted on the test window (D-039, D-040); the rolling baseline is the comparison behind the prop results below, and the calibration adoptions were re-decided under D-043.
- **Pre-registration.** The analysis designs were committed before the corresponding test results: the efficiency segments (D-037), the SGA templates (D-047), the Pillar C specification (D-051), the Phase 6b levers (D-055), the in-play model (D-056) and the shadow log (D-058). Every experiment, including rejected ones, is in `experiments/registry.jsonl`.
- **Multiple testing.** Benjamini-Hochberg at ten per cent false discovery within each pre-registered family; only survivors are called findings.

## 1. Pillar A: pre-match market efficiency and model versus market (Measured)

- **Model.** Dixon-Coles per league with exponential time decay (half-life tuned on earlier seasons), weekly refits under the completion rule. Against a static Poisson model the 1X2 log loss changes by {{a_dc_vs_static}} (95% CI {{a_dc_vs_static_lo}} to {{a_dc_vs_static_hi}}) on {{a_n_matches}} matches.
- **Against the market.** The Dixon-Coles 1X2 log loss is {{a_model_ll}}; the de-vigged Pinnacle closing line scores {{a_market_ll}}. The market is better.
- **Encompassing.** In a log-linear pool of model and market, the model's weight is {{a_enc_weight}} (95% CI {{a_enc_lo}} to {{a_enc_hi}}). The pool is compared with the recalibrated market alone so that the market's own sharpening is not credited to the model.
- **Does the line move toward the model?** The slope of the move (close minus an early snapshot taken roughly one to three days before kick-off and not timestamped, D-024) on the disagreement (model minus early) is {{a_move_slope}} (95% CI {{a_move_lo}} to {{a_move_hi}}). No.
- **Efficiency map.** {{a_n_claims}} pre-registered claims were tested in the confirmatory season. Of the {{a_repl_total}} that survived the multiple-testing control, {{a_repl_n}} reproduce in the replication season (same sign, p below one in twenty, uncorrected; the replication season is measured against the market-average close because Pinnacle prices stop in early 2026); they are model-versus-market gaps. The Bundesliga home-win bias does not replicate (p = {{a_d1_repl_p}}). The favourite-longshot slope at the close is {{a_flb_slope}} (95% CI {{a_flb_lo}} to {{a_flb_hi}}).
- **Cold start.** A promoted-team prior lowers log loss on promoted teams' first matches by {{a_cold_est}} (95% CI upper bound {{a_cold_hi}}); the interval reaches zero, so it is not adopted as a default.
- **Honest summary.** The model is a structural source of probabilities for markets without prices (totals, BTTS, props); it is not an edge against the 1X2 close.

## 2. Participation and player props, 2015/16 (Measured)

- **Starter probability** (opening state): log loss {{p4_start_ll}} against {{p4_start_base_ll}} for "started last match" (paired difference {{p4_start_diff}}). Bench appearance was under-predicted (calibration error {{p4_bench_ece_raw}}); a level recalibration chosen inside the tuning window lowers it to {{p4_bench_ece_recal}} (D-044).
- **Props** (shots 1+, 2+, 3+, shots on target 1+, 2+, anytime scorer), lineups state, against the Phase 2 rolling baseline: {{p4_props_better}} of six markets improve after multiple-testing control (anytime scorer {{p4_anytime_diff}} log loss); shots 1+ is a null ({{p4_shots1_diff}}). Settlement follows D-030 (void if the player does not appear). No prop odds exist, so only probability quality is assessed.

## 3. Simulator and same-game accumulators (Simulated, validated on realised outcomes)

- **Design.** Per simulation: scoreline from Dixon-Coles, team shots conditional on goals, minutes from the participation model (confirmed lineups, or sampled starters in the opening state), shots allocated to on-pitch players, goals assigned to shots, goals on target, other shots on target at the player's rate. {{s_n_sims}} seeded simulations per match and state; {{s_n_matches}} test matches in each state; invariants (player goals plus own goals equal team goals; goals at most shots on target at most shots; off-pitch players record nothing) are violated {{s_inv_violations}} times. Dismissals are not simulated as events (assumed).
- **Marginals.** Simulator player marginals tie with the standalone models: {{s_compB_survivors}} markets differ after multiple-testing control.
- **Joint validation (the headline).** Thirteen SGA templates, fixed before the test window, with the named players chosen by a mechanical rule, were priced as the product of the legs and as the simulated joint, and scored on realised outcomes. The simulated joint is better in {{j_better}} of {{j_n_tests}} tests and worse in {{j_worse}}. The named players are each team's mechanically chosen top scorers. Pooled (lineups) log loss {{j_sim_ll}} against {{j_naive_ll}}; calibration error {{j_sim_ece}} against {{j_naive_ece}}; the gain is {{j_2_diff}} for two-leg and {{j_3plus_diff}} for three-or-more-leg combinations.
- **What this does and does not show.** Both prices use the simulator's own marginals, so the comparison isolates the dependence structure. The simulated joint still under-predicts several multi-player templates in absolute terms (T10: {{j_t10_sim}} against a realised {{j_t10_rate}}; the naive product gives {{j_t10_naive}}). A shared match-level frailty was tested (adopted: {{fr_adopted}}).

## 4. Pillar C: lineup shock and adverse selection (upper bound; Simulated bettor)

- **Shock (UPPER BOUND: lineups assumed known with certainty one hour before kick-off).** The fair odds of {{c_shock_rot}} of rotation-risk players' prop prices move by more than a quarter between the opening and lineups states ({{c_shock_rot50}} by more than a half), against {{c_shock_nailed}} for nailed starters (more than a quarter).
- **Value of information (Measured).** The lineups price has the lower log loss in {{c_voi_markets}} of six prop markets (shots 1+: {{c_voi_shots1}}) and in {{c_voi_sga}} of thirteen SGA templates after multiple-testing control.
- **Informed bettor (SIMULATION).** A bettor who knows the lineups bets the opening price wherever his probability exceeds the quoted break-even. At a five per cent margin the expected profit per settled proposition (the Pillar C basis, not comparable with Phase 6b) is {{c_inf5}} units; replacing the lineups probabilities by an independent re-simulation (pure Monte Carlo noise) gives {{c_ctrl5}}.
- **Levers a book actually has (Phase 6b, SIMULATION).** On the Phase 6b basis (every in-squad proposition offered; void ones count zero), at a ten per cent overround, the informed expected profit per offered proposition is {{b_base}} under void-on-appearance settlement; settlement {{b_rule}} (void if the player does not start) alone gives {{b_start}}, the combination {{b_reco_name}} gives {{b_reco}}, an odds cap gives {{b_cap}}, and withdrawing rotation-risk props gives {{b_offer}} while keeping {{b_offer_kept}} of the offer. A proportional-margin "neutralising" policy was rejected as not economic (D-053). No customer demand model exists.

## 5. Pillar B: in-play team markets (Measured against a naive baseline; no market comparison)

- **Model.** Dixon-Coles intensities times a time-of-match profile on the elapsed-seconds clock, a score-state multiplier and own and opponent dismissal multipliers, fitted by penalised Poisson regression on matchweeks 1 to 19, priced by a Markov evolution of the remaining goals with a fitted match-length distribution. Future dismissals are not modelled (assumed).
- **Against the Phase 2 naive baseline** on {{i_n_matches}} test matches: better in {{i_better}} of {{i_n_tests}} checkpoint-by-market tests after multiple-testing control, worse in {{i_worse}}. The final-1X2 log-loss difference (model minus baseline; negative is better) is {{i_60_1x2}} at 60 minutes and {{i_dismiss}} after a dismissal (95% CI {{i_dismiss_lo}} to {{i_dismiss_hi}}, {{i_dismiss_n}} propositions; the baseline is naive). Pooled 1X2 calibration error {{i_ece_model}} against {{i_ece_base}}.
- **Remaining goals.** Observed {{i_rem_obs}}, model {{i_rem_model}}, baseline {{i_rem_base}}: both under-predict. After a dismissal at 75 minutes: observed {{i_75d_obs}}, model {{i_75d_model}}, baseline {{i_75d_base}}. With a two-goal lead at 45 minutes the model still under-predicts: observed {{i_45t_obs}}, model {{i_45t_model}}.
- **No market.** The data contain no in-play market prices (D-017); nothing here compares the in-play model with a market.

## 6. Product, shadow log and engineering evidence

- **API and app.** `uv run edgeforge serve api` and `serve app` replay 16 test matches through the lifecycle with margins (proportional, power, odds ratio) and SGA pricing; the evidence tab and `/model-health` show the findings above with their source files.
- **Shadow log (D-058, pre-match only, no betting).** Frozen Dixon-Coles predictions are appended to `artifacts/shadow/log.jsonl` before kick-off and committed; `shadow settle` compares them with the closing market: Pinnacle where the file has it, otherwise the market average, which is what the current football-data files will give (D-059). The log is empty so far and its commits are only external proof once pushed. Status: {{sh_logged}} logged, {{sh_settled}} settled. {{sh_verdict}}. The expectation recorded in advance is that the market is better.
- **Engineering.** A scripted fresh-clone check (ruff, mypy, pytest) last passed at commit `{{ci_sha}}` with {{ci_passed}} tests (GitHub Actions is unavailable; D-046). A Dockerfile and Cloud Run scripts exist; the image was not built here (no Docker engine) and nothing is deployed.

## 7. Limitations

- One season of player data; matchweeks 20 to 38 only for tests; simulations are conditional on the model being right about minutes and shot shares.
- The shock and informed-bettor results assume lineups are known with certainty and that bets are accepted at the opening price without limits.
- No prop or in-play prices exist, so no market comparison is possible for props, SGAs or in-play markets.
- Player names and raw event data are not stored in the repository (data licence, D-028, D-041).

